Here are proven approaches you’ve already used elsewhere in your stack, adapted specifically to keep your language detection service (Node.js 18 + franc v6.1.0 + Redis) responsive as concurrency grows:

- Scale out and autoscale
  - Run multiple replicas behind a load balancer (same pattern you used on AWS Fargate for the chatbot). Add autoscaling on CPU or p95 latency so it scales up before saturation.
  - If you use Kubernetes, reuse the HPA pattern you’ve already applied (min/max replicas with CPU or custom latency metrics).

- Use multi-tier caching
  - Keep Redis (you’ve been using 5–10 min TTLs) and add a tiny in-process LRU cache before Redis for ultra-hot keys to avoid network hops.
  - Normalize inputs (trim, lowercase, strip markup) to maximize cache hits; tune TTLs based on your hit rate (you measured ~70% cache hits in April—aim higher).
  - Prevent cache stampedes (set a short lock key or singleflight so only one worker computes a miss).

- Reduce work per request
  - Preprocess text (you already do) and cap the analyzed text length to a reasonable maximum.
  - Narrow franc’s search space where possible (e.g., tenant- or product-specific language whitelists; enforce a minimum text length to avoid low-confidence guesses).
  - Add a batch endpoint (accept an array of texts) to amortize per-request overhead during bulk operations.

- Concurrency on a single host
  - Use Node’s cluster or worker_threads to utilize all CPU cores for franc (CPU-bound) without blocking the event loop for I/O.
  - Example (cluster, minimal):
    const cluster = require('cluster');
    const os = require('os');
    if (cluster.isMaster) {
      os.cpus().forEach(() => cluster.fork());
    } else {
      // start your Express app here
    }

- Backpressure, shedding, and SLAs
  - Add rate limiting and quick timeouts so slow clients or bursts don’t degrade everyone (you’ve used express-rate-limit and circuit breakers elsewhere—apply the same pattern).
  - When overwhelmed, return 429/503 with Retry-After rather than letting latency climb.

- Hardening and fallbacks
  - Apply the circuit breaker you already use with GPT-4 to Redis calls; fall back to in-process cache if Redis is degraded.
  - Set strict request size limits and validate inputs to avoid expensive work on bad/oversized payloads.
  - Keep HTTP keep-alive enabled between services; avoid compression for tiny JSON.

- Observability-driven tuning
  - Track p50/p95/p99 latency, CPU, memory, Redis hit rate, and per-replica utilization (you’re already using Winston logs and AWS X-Ray—extend to this service).
  - Use those metrics to tune: cache TTLs, max text length, autoscaling thresholds, and replica counts.

- Example: in-process LRU + Redis cache in the route
  const LRU = require('lru-cache');
  const redis = require('redis');
  const franc = require('franc');
  const lru = new LRU({ max: 5000, ttl: 1000 * 60 }); // ~1 min hot cache
  const r = redis.createClient();

  app.post('/detect', async (req, res) => {
    const raw = (req.body.text || '').toString();
    const text = raw.toLowerCase().replace(/<[^>]+>/g, '').replace(/[^\w\s]/g, ' ').trim().slice(0, 500); // cap length
    if (!text) return res.status(400).json({ error: 'Missing text' });

    const key = `lang:${text}`;
    const hot = lru.get(key);
    if (hot) return res.json({ language: hot });

    const cached = await r.get(key);
    if (cached) {
      lru.set(key, cached);
      return res.json({ language: cached });
    }

    const lang = franc(text);
    lru.set(key, lang);
    await r.setEx(key, 300, lang); // 5 min
    return res.json({ language: lang });
  });

Quick checklist to keep p95 low as load rises:
- 2–10 replicas with autoscaling (CPU or p95)
- In-process LRU before Redis; tune TTLs
- Cap input length; whitelist likely languages
- Cluster/worker_threads to use all cores
- Rate limiting + fast timeouts + circuit breakers
- Monitor p95/p99, CPU, Redis hit rate; iterate

If you share your current p95/p99, CPU per replica, and Redis hit rate under peak, I can suggest concrete thresholds for autoscaling and cache TTLs tailored to your traffic.
